    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A protein pint made in a Ninja Creami lives or dies on the powder. Regular ice cream stays soft because of sugar and fat; a protein base has little of either, so the wrong powder freezes into an icy block that spins into dust. Casein and whey-casein blends hold water and thicken the base, which is why they make the creamiest pints. Here are six powders worth using, chosen on protein type, label and buyer feedback, and the simple technique that turns a first-spin crumble into something that tastes like dessert.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="why-powder-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Protein Powder Makes or Breaks a Creami Pint
          </h2>

          <p>
            The Ninja Creami works differently from a churning ice cream maker. You freeze a liquid base solid in a pint container, usually for about a day, and the machine's blade shaves and whips the frozen block into a soft texture. That means the base has to freeze into something the blade can turn creamy. In ordinary ice cream, sugar lowers the freezing point and fat coats ice crystals, so the block stays slightly soft and the result is smooth.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein ice cream removes most of that sugar and fat, which is the point, but it also removes what keeps ice crystals small. A base of milk, water and a thin whey isolate freezes into hard, coarse crystals. The first spin turns it into something closer to snow or powder, and owners often describe it as dusty or crumbly. This is the most common complaint in protein Creami recipes, and it is mostly a powder and ratio problem rather than a machine problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein powder can fix what it causes. Casein forms a thicker, gel-like mix with liquid and binds water, so less of it freezes into large crystals. Whey-casein blends do a milder version of the same thing, and many contain gums that act as stabilizers. Pick the right powder, add a small amount of thickener, and the same pint that would have been icy comes out dense and scoopable.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Casein and casein blends make thicker pints than whey isolate.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">They bind water and thicken the base, which keeps ice crystals small. Add a pinch of xanthan or guar gum, freeze for about a day, and expect to re-spin once with a splash of milk.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not freeze and spin pints for this guide. The picks come from label analysis, protein type and the large body of public feedback from Creami owners about which powders give thick or icy results, using the method on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test page</a>. Any comment about texture or taste is buyer feedback reported as such, not our own result.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein type carried the most weight. Pure caseins and whey-casein blends ranked highest because of how they behave in a frozen base. Whey blends that buyers consistently say work well with a thickener came next. We also weighed flavor range, since a Creami pint is dessert and a flavor you enjoy matters more here than in a post-workout shake, and protein per scoop, so that a pint delivers a meaningful dose for its calories.
          </p>

          <p>
            We favored widely sold products with long track records and full ingredient disclosure. We did not reward novelty ingredients, fat burners or proprietary blends. If you already own a powder that is not on this list, the same principles apply: a casein or blend will usually freeze thicker than an isolate, and a thin powder can be rescued with a thickener and a re-spin.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Protein Powders for Ninja Creami, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            The picks are ordered by how reliably they produce a thick, creamy pint with minimal fuss. Nutrition figures are per the label at the time of writing and vary by flavor. Comments on texture and taste summarize what Creami owners widely report in public reviews and forums; we did not run our own freezer trials. Ninja and Creami are trademarks of SharkNinja, which has no connection to this guide and has not endorsed any product here.
          </p>

