        <div id="casein-vs-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Casein, Blends, Whey and Plant: What Each Does in the Pint
          </h2>

          <p>
            Casein is the closest thing to a shortcut. Because it thickens liquids and holds water, a casein base freezes into a denser block that spins into something like soft-serve or gelato. Many owners report that a casein pint needs only one re-spin and little or no added gum. The trade-off is flavor: casein can taste slightly chalky, so chocolate, peanut butter and cookie flavors hide it better than plain vanilla. Our <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">best casein protein powder</a> guide covers casein beyond the freezer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whey-casein blends are the most popular middle ground. They taste closer to a milkshake than pure casein, still thicken the base and often include gums already. Whey concentrate and standard whey blends work too, but they freeze thinner, so most owners add xanthan gum or a spoonful of pudding mix. Whey isolate, especially clear or hydrolyzed isolate, gives the iciest results and is the hardest to make creamy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant powders are workable but less predictable. Pea and pea-rice blends absorb water and can freeze thick, though some owners find them gritty or earthy in a frozen dessert. Adding a little fat, such as a spoonful of nut butter or using a richer plant milk, helps. Dairy-free readers can find more options in our <a href="/best-dairy-free-protein-powder/">best dairy-free protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

