        <div id="base-recipe" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. A Simple Protein Ice Cream Base, and Why Each Part Is There
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most protein Creami recipes shared by owners follow the same template. Start with roughly 1.25 to 1.5 cups of milk, leaving room below the pint's max fill line, then add one scoop of casein or a blend, a sweetener to taste and a thickener. Ultra-filtered milk such as Fairlife is a common choice because it adds extra protein and less sugar than regular milk. Blend the base with a blender or frother rather than stirring, because clumps of dry powder freeze into gritty pockets.
          </p>

          <p>
            Freeze the pint level on a flat shelf. Ninja's own guidance is to freeze the base for about 24 hours, and owners widely report that a shorter freeze gives a softer core and uneven texture. A pint that freezes at an angle or with a hump in the middle spins unevenly. If your freezer is very cold, let the pint sit on the counter for five to ten minutes before spinning, which many owners say reduces the powdery first-spin result.
          </p>

          <p>
            On machines that have it, run the Lite Ice Cream program, which product listings describe as designed for bases made with sugar substitutes or low-fat milk. Expect the first spin to look crumbly. Add one to two tablespoons of milk to the center, run Re-spin, and check again. Many protein pints need one re-spin and some need two. Mix-ins go in last using the mix-in program. For a full <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-baking/">baking with protein powder</a> walkthrough, which follows different rules, see our baking guide.
