        <div id="thickeners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Xanthan, Guar and Pudding Mix: The Thickeners Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            Xanthan gum and guar gum are the two thickeners owners use most. Both are used in commercial ice cream as stabilizers, and both bind water so that ice crystals stay small. The amounts are tiny, around an eighth to a quarter of a teaspoon per pint. Too much gives a slimy, stretchy or gummy texture, so start small. Whisk or blend the gum into the liquid thoroughly, because a clump will not dissolve once frozen.
          </p>

          <p>
            Instant sugar-free pudding mix is the other popular trick. A tablespoon or two thickens the base and adds flavor, which is why vanilla, cheesecake and chocolate pudding mixes are common in shared recipes. Read the label, though. Pudding mixes contain modified starch, and many sugar-free versions are sweetened with aspartame and acesulfame potassium, so people with phenylketonuria must avoid aspartame. The mix also adds some carbohydrate, which matters on a strict low-carb diet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gums are the leaner option, pudding the more flavorful one. If either upsets your stomach, as gums and sugar alcohols can for some people in larger amounts, a casein-heavy base with a little more fat, such as a spoonful of cream cheese, Greek yogurt or nut butter, can thicken the pint instead. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-sensitive-stomach/">best protein powder for sensitive stomach</a> guide covers ingredients that commonly cause trouble.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fix-texture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Fixing Powdery, Icy or Gummy Pints
          </h2>

          <p>
            A powdery or crumbly first spin is normal for protein bases and is not a failure. It means the base is very cold and low in sugar and fat. A tablespoon or two of milk and a re-spin usually fixes it. If you routinely need three re-spins, the base needs more body: switch to casein or a blend, add a pinch of gum, or let the pint temper on the counter for a few minutes before the first spin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Icy pints, where you can see and taste crystals, come from too much water relative to protein, fat and thickener. Swap some water for milk, use a thicker powder or add a quarter teaspoon of xanthan. Gummy or stretchy pints are the opposite: too much gum or pudding mix. Halve the thickener next time. A pint that is soupy after spinning usually did not freeze long enough or was spun too many times; refreeze it and spin again.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leftover pints freeze hard again in the freezer, so smooth the top before refreezing and expect to re-spin before eating. Follow Ninja's own instructions for the machine, including the max fill line and freezing the pint level, because an overfilled or uneven pint can strain the machine. Owners report that most texture problems come down to the same three levers: powder type, thickener and re-spinning with a splash of liquid.
          </p>

