        <div id="flavors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Flavored Powders and Mix-Ins: What Buyers Use
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cold dulls sweetness and flavor, so a powder that tastes strong in a shaker can taste muted frozen. That is why dessert-style flavors are so popular for protein ice cream. Buyers widely report that cookie, cereal and cake flavors from brands such as Ghost translate well, and that chocolate and peanut butter flavors hide casein's chalkiness best. Vanilla is the most flexible base for adding your own flavors, from cocoa and espresso to fruit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mix-ins should go in after the main spin using the mix-in program, so they stay in pieces. Crushed cookies, chopped protein bars, berries, peanut butter powder and dark chocolate are common. Fresh fruit blended into the base adds sugar and water, which can make the pint icier, so many owners prefer adding fruit as a mix-in or using frozen fruit purée in measured amounts.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want a pint that tastes as close to real ice cream as possible, accept a little more fat from whole or 2 percent milk or a spoonful of cream. If protein per calorie matters most, as it does on a cut, stick with fat-free ultra-filtered milk and a casein base. Our <a href="/best-tasting-protein-powder/">best tasting protein powder</a> guide covers the flavors buyers actually finish, and many owners also use a ready-to-drink shake as the entire base; our <a href="/best-protein-drink/">best protein drink</a> guide compares those.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="nutrition" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Much Protein Is in a Pint, and Is It Worth It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A typical protein pint made with about 1.25 cups of fat-free ultra-filtered milk and one scoop of casein lands at roughly 40 g of protein and somewhere around 220 to 260 calories before mix-ins, based on common label values. A standard pint of premium ice cream can carry several times those calories with a fraction of the protein. That gap is why protein ice cream has become so popular with people tracking calories.
          </p>

          <p>
            The numbers rise quickly with mix-ins, whole milk, pudding mix and sauces, so log what you actually add. A pint with cookies and peanut butter can double its calories. If you are eating protein ice cream as part of a weight-loss plan, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-loss/">best protein powder for weight loss</a> guide explains how to fit protein into a calorie target, and our <a href="/best-low-carb-protein-powder/">best low-carb protein powder</a> guide helps if carbohydrates are your limit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein ice cream is a useful tool, not a health food. It is a good way to reach a daily protein target with something that feels like a treat, and it can replace a higher-sugar dessert. Sweeteners, gums and sugar alcohols can cause bloating in some people when eaten by the pint. Anyone managing diabetes, kidney disease or another medical condition should check sweetener and protein amounts with a doctor or registered dietitian.
          </p>
        </div>

