        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using whey isolate and expecting ice cream</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Isolate is thin and lean, so it freezes into coarse ice crystals and spins into powder. If isolate is all you have, add xanthan gum or pudding mix and plan on re-spinning. For thick pints without fuss, casein or a whey-casein blend is the better buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stirring instead of blending the base</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dry protein clumps do not dissolve once frozen and leave gritty pockets. Blend the base or use a frother so the powder and any gum are fully hydrated, then let it sit for a few minutes to release foam before pouring it into the pint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overdoing the xanthan gum</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An eighth to a quarter teaspoon is enough for a pint. More than that gives a slimy, stretchy texture that many owners mistake for a powder problem. Start small, and increase only if the pint is still icy after a re-spin.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the re-spin</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A crumbly first spin is normal for low-sugar protein bases. Add one to two tablespoons of milk to the center and run Re-spin. Most protein pints need one, and some need two, before they turn creamy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Freezing too briefly or unevenly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ninja recommends freezing the base for about a day. Short freezes leave a soft core, and a pint frozen at an angle spins unevenly. Freeze it level on a flat shelf and stay below the max fill line marked on the pint.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

